## Objective
Add `--fzf` flag to the search command that pipes results to fzf for interactive selection.

## Implementation Steps

### 1. Update search command options
In `src/commands/search.ts`:

```typescript
.option("--fzf", "Pipe results to fzf for interactive selection", false)
```

### 2. Implement fzf flow
```typescript
// After getting search results
if (options.fzf) {
  const fzf = new FzfPipe();

  // Check if fzf is available
  if (!await FzfPipe.isAvailable()) {
    console.error("Error: fzf is not installed. Install it from https://github.com/junegunn/fzf");
    process.exitCode = 1;
    return;
  }

  // Transform results for fzf
  const fzfResults = response.data.map(chunk => ({
    path: chunk.metadata.path,
    startLine: chunk.generated_metadata.start_line + 1,
    endLine: chunk.generated_metadata.start_line + chunk.generated_metadata.num_lines,
    score: chunk.score,
    preview: chunk.text.split("\n")[0]
  }));

  // Let user select
  const selection = await fzf.selectWithFzf(fzfResults);

  if (selection?.selected) {
    await fzf.openInEditor(selection.filePath, selection.lineNumber);
  } else {
    console.log("No selection made");
  }

  return;  // Skip normal output
}

// Normal output continues below...
```

### 3. Handle edge cases
- Empty results: Show message instead of empty fzf
- Single result: Consider auto-selecting?
- Very large result sets: May need pagination

### 4. Environment considerations
- Respect $EDITOR environment variable
- Respect $VISUAL as fallback
- Default to vi if neither set

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] `mgrep search --fzf "query"` opens fzf with results
- [ ] Selecting result opens $EDITOR at correct line
- [ ] ESC/Ctrl+C cancels gracefully
- [ ] Error message when fzf not installed
- [ ] Works with vim, nvim, code, nano
- [ ] Empty results handled gracefully

